=== SURVEY ===

Baseline /settings/safety surface (pre-slice):

  - Route does NOT exist. The closest match in App.tsx is a
    `/safety-settings` route (vehicle telemetry — seatbelts, lock
    state) that is unrelated. This slice introduces a new
    /settings/safety route under the Settings family.
  - `useSettings()` (web/src/hooks/useSettings.ts) returns
    `{ settings: AppSettings, ... }` where AppSettings (from
    web/src/api/types.ts) has the safety-related fields:
        api_suspended:           boolean
        quiet_hours_enabled:     boolean
        quiet_hours_start:       string | undefined
        quiet_hours_end:         string | undefined
        alert_digest_mode:       string
        critical_flash_enabled?: boolean
        tab_badge_enabled?:      boolean
  - `internal/database/settings_repo.go` `settingsDefaults()` is the
    canonical source-of-truth for the default values when a column
    has never been written. The handler's
    `projectSafetySettingsEnvelope` MUST mirror those defaults when
    the row is missing.
  - `internal/api/router.go` registers `/settings` GET/PUT via
    `aiSettingsRepo` (used by the existing /settings page); reused
    here for the AI source adapter.

Baseline behavior preserved:
  - The existing /settings page continues to surface every setting
    (including the safety-related ones) via the standard form. This
    slice does NOT remove anything from /settings.
  - The new /settings/safety page renders the SAME data via a
    deterministic listing, with each row's docs link pointing back to
    the per-area reference docs (notifications/quiet-hours,
    notifications/digest, notifications/tab-signalling,
    operations/api-suspended).
  - Off-mode test (Go side: `TestSafetySettingExplainerAIOffShowsStaticHelpOnly`)
    proves POST /api/v1/ai/settings/safety/explain returns 404 when
    `ai_mode='off'` and that GET /api/v1/settings (the baseline)
    is still reachable.

Off-mode test name pinning: the React test file MUST be named
`TestSafetySettingExplainerAIOffShowsStaticHelpOnly.test.tsx` (and
the Go test function MUST be named identically) because the slice's
verification command is
`npm test -- --run TestSafetySettingExplainerAIOffShowsStaticHelpOnly`,
where the positional pattern is matched against the file PATH.

=== REASONING ===

ADR-015 (AI-Off Contract) preservation:

  - I3 baseline intact: SafetyPage's deterministic `<ul>` of every
    safety setting is rendered from `useSettings()` and is fully
    usable when `ai_mode='off'`. The AI explainer card is added
    ABOVE the list, never replaces it.
  - I4 zero egress: the strategy uses PolicyChatbot (Allow=nil) via
    redactadapter.Wrap — every typed envelope projection drops PII
    before it leaves the process boundary.
  - I5 hidden UI: AISafetySettingExplainer is wrapped with
    `withAiFeature('safety-setting-explainer', InnerSection)` so the
    section is ABSENT (returns null) in off mode, not greyed out.
  - I6 404 routes: the backend route is mounted via
    `g.Wrap("safety-setting-explainer", h.ServeHTTP)` and the
    foundation guard returns 404 when the per-feature toggle is off
    (proven by Go-side
    `TestSafetySettingExplainerAIOffShowsStaticHelpOnly`).
  - I7 per-feature: the off-mode React test sets the per-feature
    toggle TRUE and `ai_mode='off'` to defeat the trivial
    "everything off" path. The AI section MUST still be hidden.
  - I10 type system: every typed surface (the strategy's
    `SafetySettingsEnvelope`, the `SafetySettingDescriptor` map,
    the handler's request/response, the `query_safety_settings`
    tool's input schema) is a typed Go struct. The LLM never
    constructs SQL.

P1-P10 alignment:
  - P1 provider port via `internal/ai/provider`, used through the
    decorator chain. No direct imports from feature code.
  - P4 dispatcher loop: the foundation handler (already shipped)
    runs the strategy through the locked decorator order
    (redaction → rate limit → cost cap → audit → trace).
  - P6 backend guard: `g.Wrap("safety-setting-explainer", …)` at
    the route boundary; the foundation guard implementation
    short-circuits to 404 in off mode.
  - P6 frontend guard: `withAiFeature` HOC + `useAiEnabled`
    hook — both shipped in foundation slices.
  - P8 eval goldens: 3 deterministic goldens (NEEDS at least 3,
    delivered exactly 3).
  - P9 feature registry: full route metadata (Backend, Frontend,
    UITestIDs, JobNames=[], PushKinds=[]).

W1 inline wiring: the SPA component
`web/src/components/ai/AISafetySettingExplainer.tsx` calls
`useAiStream({ url: '/ai/settings/safety/explain', body: {} })`
unconditionally at the top of the function (Hooks rules satisfied),
the primary action's `disabled` is computed inside AIFeatureCard
from `canStart && stream.state === 'streaming'` (no literal
`disabled={true}`), and a dedicated `useEffect` calls `cancel()` on
unmount. Render contract is NARRATIVE — the strategy never emits a
typed `tool_result` that would require an "Apply to form" button,
so the component does not surface one.

HX (Helix UX): AISafetySettingExplainer renders through the shared
`AIFeatureCard` scaffold (NOT a bespoke GlassPanel + Button +
AiOutputPanel composition). The per-feature verb
"Explain my settings" is passed as `buttonLabel`; the card's CTA
accessible name composes to "Ask Helix · Explain my settings".
User-visible i18n copy (added under `safetySettings.aiExplainer.*`)
says "Helix" not "AI" in the description. Tests locate the CTA via
the unanchored regex `/Explain my settings/i`.

P11/P12 (wired-or-absent + no placeholder buttons): the SPA
component is wired end-to-end. The W1 self-check
(`Select-String -Path 'web/src/components/ai/AISafetySettingExplainer.tsx' -Pattern 'future slice|coming soon|wiring lands|would call POST' | Measure-Object` →
0) is clean for THIS slice's file. (The project-wide W1 self-check
returns 1 because of pre-existing copy in
`web/src/components/ai/AIChatbotIndicator.tsx`, which slice 0065 W1
will sweep.)

Tools selected: `query_safety_settings` (NEW) + `retrieve_docs`
(REUSED from `internal/ai/tools/help.go`). Both are read-only typed
tools; no write paths. The query tool returns the full envelope
every call so the LLM can prove "out of scope" by checking key
absence in the deterministic key set.

Retrieval: `retrieve_docs` is bound to the `docs` corpus only —
no `settings_schema` source. The settings schema is exposed
deterministically via the typed `query_safety_settings` envelope,
which is the F7 retrieval design's preferred path for narrow
typed surfaces (see methodology rubber-duck note: avoid widening
retrieve_docs to settings_schema when a typed envelope suffices).

